  "activity": {
    "id": "dna_replication_activity",
    "name": "DNA Structure and Replication Lab Activity",
    "description": "An interactive AR exploration of DNA structure, base-pairing rules, antiparallel orientation, and semiconservative replication. Students progress through schematic and realistic models, engage in base-pairing activities, observe the replication fork at work, and explore the semiconservative nature of DNA replication.",
    "workplace": "dna_replication_lab",
    "start": "step_1_dna_ladder",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_1_dna_ladder",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction: DNA as a Ladder Schematic",
          "description": "Begin with a flat, ladder-like representation of DNA. Two vertical lines represent the sugar-phosphate backbones, and horizontal rungs between them represent base pairs, each colored by base identity (adenine red, thymine blue, guanine yellow, cytosine green). This scene establishes the regular repeating structure of DNA and the pattern of complementary base pairing before introducing geometric complexity. You will observe how the bases project inward and pair with their complements on the opposite strand."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_ladder_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_ladder_image",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "dna_ladder_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "intro_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "dna_ladder_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "dna_ladder_image"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_1_dna_ladder"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_2_helix_transition",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Transformation: The DNA Double Helix",
          "description": "Watch the ladder structure twist into the familiar double-helix shape. The sugar-phosphate backbones spiral around a common axis while base pairs stack along the interior. You can toggle between the schematic ladder and the realistic helix representation at any time to build your mental mapping. The helix completes one full turn approximately every ten base pairs, and you can observe the major and minor grooves as geometric features of the structure."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ladder_to_helix_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "intro_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ladder_to_helix_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_2_helix_transition"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_3_antiparallel",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Orientation: Antiparallel Strands and 5' to 3' Directionality",
          "description": "The realistic helix is now displayed with arrows along each sugar-phosphate backbone indicating direction: one strand's arrow points up, the other points down. Labels at the ends of each strand identify the 5' and 3' termini, which refer to specific carbon atoms on the sugar ring. This antiparallel arrangement\u2014where the two strands run in opposite directions\u2014is not optional; it is a direct consequence of the directional chemistry of the sugar-phosphate backbone. You can rotate the helix to confirm the directional arrows from every angle and understand why both strands cannot run the same way."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "direction_arrows_animation",
              "poi": "strand_5_prime"
            },
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "direction_arrows_animation",
              "poi": "strand_3_prime"
            },
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "antiparallel_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "dna_helix_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "direction_arrows_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_3_antiparallel"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_4_base_pairing_activity",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Interactive Activity: Complete the Complementary Base-Pair Sequence",
          "description": "A partial ladder structure appears: one complete strand on the left with bases labeled (for example, 5'-ATGCCAGT-3'), and an empty strand on the right with backbone present but no bases. Loose bases (A, T, G, C) are arranged in a palette below. Drag bases from the palette and attach them to the empty strand to complete the complementary sequence. Correct pairings (A with T, G with C) snap into place and display hydrogen bonds as dashed lines\u2014two lines for A-T pairs, three lines for G-C pairs. Incorrect pairings are rejected. After completing the strand, the narration confirms the new sequence and emphasizes that the new strand runs 3' to 5', opposite to the template, and that hydrogen bond count reflects pair strength."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "base_pairing_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "base_pairing_activity_image",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "base_pairing_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "base_pairing_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "base_pairing_workspace",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "hydrogen_bond_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_4_base_pairing_activity"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_5_helicase_unwinds",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Replication Begins: Helicase Unwinds the Double Helix",
          "description": "The scene returns to a section of the realistic double helix and introduces helicase, the first enzyme in replication. Helicase appears at one end of the helix, positioned and labeled. Watch it move along the DNA, breaking the hydrogen bonds between paired bases and opening up the double helix. Behind the moving helicase, the two strands are separated but no other proteins are present yet. Helicase's function is critical: it makes the bases accessible so replication can proceed. Understanding helicase's action is the first step to understanding the complete replication fork."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "helicase_animation",
              "poi": "helicase_site"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "helicase_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "helicase_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_5_helicase_unwinds"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_6_ssb_proteins",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Stabilization: Single-Strand Binding Proteins Protect Exposed Strands",
          "description": "As the replication fork progresses and the two strands separate behind helicase, single-strand binding proteins (SSBs) appear and attach to each exposed strand. The SSBs are rendered as small labeled spheres decorating the separated strands. Single strands of DNA are inherently unstable\u2014they tend to re-pair or fold back on themselves. The SSBs hold the strands open and accessible, preventing them from collapsing back together and ensuring that the replication machinery can access the template bases. Without SSBs, replication would stall."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ssb_proteins_animation",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ssb_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ssb_proteins_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_6_ssb_proteins"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_7_leading_strand",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Synthesis: The Leading Strand Grows Continuously",
          "description": "DNA polymerase now appears on one of the two exposed template strands\u2014specifically the one oriented so that synthesis proceeds in the same direction as the moving replication fork. DNA polymerase can only build a new strand by adding nucleotides to its 3' end, so it can only travel along a template strand in the 3' to 5' direction, building the new strand 5' to 3'. On the leading strand template, the polymerase moves continuously, following the fork. Free nucleotides appear from solution, pair with the template bases, and are added to the growing new strand. This continuous synthesis on the leading strand is elegant and efficient\u2014the polymerase keeps pace with the fork."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "leading_strand_animation",
              "poi": "leading_strand_site"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "leading_strand_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "leading_strand_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_7_leading_strand"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_8_lagging_strand_ligase",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Asymmetry: The Lagging Strand Grows in Fragments and Is Sealed by Ligase",
          "description": "On the other exposed template strand\u2014the one whose 3' end points away from the moving fork\u2014synthesis cannot proceed continuously. DNA polymerase tries to approach the fork but is forced to work backward, away from it, producing only a short fragment before it must release and jump back to the fork to start again. This cycle repeats, creating a series of short pieces called Okazaki fragments along the lagging strand template, growing in the opposite direction from the leading strand. After several fragments have been laid down, DNA ligase appears and seals the junctions between fragments, joining them into a continuous strand. This asymmetry\u2014continuous on one strand, discontinuous on the other\u2014arises directly from the antiparallel structure of DNA and the unidirectional activity of DNA polymerase."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "okazaki_fragments_animation",
              "poi": "lagging_strand_site"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ligase_animation",
              "poi": "lagging_strand_site"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "lagging_strand_narration"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "okazaki_fragments_animation"
            },
            {
              "target": "replication_fork_structure",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "ligase_animation"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_8_lagging_strand_ligase"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_9_complete_fork",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Integration: The Complete Replication Fork",
